WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:00.920
I grew up in India.

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.820
I had no idea or no concept of like, what is Boston?

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.460
What is New York?

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:05.700
I hadn't been to any of these places.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.920
Boston generally is a very multicultural environment.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.760
People come there from a lot of different countries,

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.540
and I went to Boston University, where that's especially the case.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.220
After graduating from college,

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:16.300
I actually started a PhD in machine learning,

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:18.040
which I actually dropped out of.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.580
I left my PhD program, and I joined a startup.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.880
People thought I was crazy that I would just drop out of it

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:24.760
and go- kind of go and join some startup, right?

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:27.080
But I think it was the right decision, looking back at it.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:28.420
It was a very high-risk decision,

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:30.600
but it worked out, especially in the US,

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.880
and I think that's what makes the US such a special place.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:36.060
There's definitely so much opportunity that I

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:38.580
think it's better to be a little bit more of a risk-taker.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:40.500
Most of the time you can take a risk

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:42.320
and actually get rewarded for it.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.220
I think people don't bias enough towards taking risks.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.040
If you just do what everybody else is doing,

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.400
then you'll just end up wherever everybody else is going, right?

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.620
My name is Gaurav, and I'm the co-founder and CEO of Captions.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.920
Captions is a video creation company.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.400
Our goal is to make it easy for anybody, anywhere to be able

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:04.920
to tell their story through video without actually going

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:07.820
through the physical process of recording or editing a video.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:10.520
We've raised over $100 million in funding.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.360
It's gotten to tens of millions of users

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:16.840
and over 100,000-plus users daily.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:25.300
I was the, in the US for the first four years of my life,

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:26.660
and my parents moved to India.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:27.680
I grew up in India.

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:29.160
Moving back to the US, like,

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.880
I had no idea or no concept of like what it would be like.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:33.780
What is Boston?

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:34.700
What is New York like?

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:36.040
I hadn't been to any of these places.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:38.240
My parents were like, "Boston's great.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:38.940
You should go there."

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:40.100
And I was like, "Sounds great.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:40.720
Let me do that."

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.080
So at Boston University,

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.480
it was an interesting situation because coming from India,

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:48.400
sort of the curriculum there, especially around computer science,

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.680
is just very advanced compared to the US,

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.220
and a lot of the things that we

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:54.060
actually were being taught in college,

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:56.340
we had already covered in high school, for the most part.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:58.860
A lot of college classes were essentially

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.180
repeating what I had already done in high school.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:04.020
It probably was the case for a lot of people from India, I imagine.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:06.440
I think that did give me a chance to actually go

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.780
and explore outside of just that and kind

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:11.640
of see what else I can learn and what more I can do.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:12.780
I did tons of projects.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.920
I, like, worked on projects with my roommates.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:17.580
I worked on a lot of different apps that we launched,

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:20.280
and this was, like, in 2012, 2011, when

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:21.400
the App Store was still early.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:23.220
In high school and going into college,

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:25.820
I had started a kind of a company in a strange way.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:27.500
I say that because I actually

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.060
didn't realize it was a company until much later.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:31.460
I was working on some games,

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:33.380
and there was some need around, like,

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:35.880
security software for those games, and I decided to make it,

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.080
and the game company decided to buy it from me,

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.020
not knowing that I was, like, 16 years old.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.120
And I ended up selling it to a bunch of other companies as well.

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:44.700
I didn't realize at that time that this is what

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:46.880
a company is, is like you make a product, you sell it.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.520
But much later I was like, "Wow, that's kind of what it was."

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:51.880
It was a interesting sort of experience to go through

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.640
because it kind of transitioned from generally games

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:57.640
and gaming into sort of more general skill set.

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:00.080
I really enjoy learning new things.

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:02.180
How do you learn things quickly,

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:03.820
and how do you figure things out quickly?

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:06.180
It's something that not a lot of people talk about,

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.560
but it's like one of the most important skills in my mind.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:09.980
Go to a new domain.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:12.920
You know, go to a new area, something that you haven't done before.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:14.160
Try it for the first time.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.720
Figure it out quickly, and be actually useful

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:18.080
and productive in it, is like...

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.240
It's a pretty cool skill, and it's a pretty fun one to work

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:22.220
on because you get to try out a lot of different things.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:23.840
You get to learn a lot of different things

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.560
and focus on that as a skill in itself.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:27.380
Being able to do all those different things

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.260
and figuring those things out is really important.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.720
Through the early part of my career, I was really focused

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:41.300
on machine learning as a central aspect of what I was doing.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:43.640
At Microsoft, I joined them on this

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:46.860
new team they were developing called Azure Machine Learning.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:48.940
It's like a machine learning platform they were building.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.380
And then at Snapchat, there was a bit of a difference.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:54.760
I got a chance to really get more on the product side,

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:57.120
and eventually I ended up joining the design team,

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:00.320
which was, like, a huge shift from what I had done previously,

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:03.520
and the idea of, like, connecting technology to actual design

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:06.040
and actual product made a lot of sense.

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:09.100
So I ended up starting a team there called Design Engineering,

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:11.780
which was like, how do you bring together great design

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:14.020
and great engineering to build amazing products, right?

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:15.860
One of the things I learned at Snap,

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:17.700
if you think about Snap as a company,

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:20.860
I think Snap was one of the earliest companies to actually

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:23.960
get into video for consumer use cases, especially short video.

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:28.180
It did get me very immersed into the space of, like,

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:30.540
video in general, especially video for communication,

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:32.300
short videos, and all the challenges,

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:34.380
both technical and product, that are related to that.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.800
But I think in general, one of the things that I noticed

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:41.500
was essentially a shift in the way that people were communicating.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:43.300
A lot of people thought TikTok was just,

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:45.660
"Oh, TikTok, that's, like, just people dancing, right?

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:46.500
It's for fun."

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:49.060
But I think the reality was that, yes,

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:50.300
there were a bunch of people dancing,

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:51.740
but there was a lot of people talking,

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:54.280
and there were a lot of people telling stories and, you know,

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:56.320
sometimes bad things that happened to them,

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:57.580
good things that happened to them.

00:04:57.640 --> 00:04:59.580
People were, like, talking about ideas and, like,

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:01.640
what could be done and what should be done,

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:04.000
and people were a- arguing with each other, right?

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:06.740
And I think that was, like, a shift in the way people communicated

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:08.540
from, like, that's the type of stuff that would happen,

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:11.060
like, on Twitter or, like, on text beforehand, right?

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:13.180
And it was now happening on video for the first time.

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:16.380
So I got pretty familiar with all the different

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:18.040
ways that people are communicating

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:20.560
and kind of what works better and what doesn't work.

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:27.040
When we started the company,

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.140
we were thinking about building a social network,

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:32.180
and so we were thinking, "Okay, there's, like, a distribution part,

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:34.360
and then there's a creation part of the social network."

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:36.940
We thought, "Hey, let's start with the creation first."

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:40.680
So we started off with the idea of literally just adding captions

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:43.760
to videos, text on videos based on what people are saying.

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:45.160
The reason we did this is because

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:47.920
we saw there was a big accessibility movement at that time.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:49.320
There were a lot of people

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:52.660
sort of manually writing text on top of their videos,

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:55.220
and they would spend an hour, like, typing it all out.

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:58.820
There wasn't actually a solution for this that was in the market

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:01.480
at that time, so we were the first ones to build it,

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:05.000
and the absolute first project, we built it in one weekend.

00:06:05.596 --> 00:06:07.716
Just as like a test essentially,

00:06:08.316 --> 00:06:09.496
and we put it on the App Store,

00:06:10.156 --> 00:06:11.336
and the next day we woke up

00:06:11.376 --> 00:06:14.036
and it was like 600 people per minute using it.

00:06:14.076 --> 00:06:16.036
Basically, it was completely at the top of the App

00:06:16.096 --> 00:06:19.736
Store with no marketing, no nothing done essentially.

00:06:20.036 --> 00:06:21.856
And I think that was like the moment where it was like,

00:06:21.916 --> 00:06:23.436
"Wow, it can't be this easy," right?

00:06:23.476 --> 00:06:24.516
Like, it, it really can't be.

00:06:24.736 --> 00:06:27.496
Because we had been told, right, like, "It's gonna be a lot

00:06:27.516 --> 00:06:29.836
of work to find product market fit," and things like that.

00:06:30.036 --> 00:06:33.016
But I think that being said, we had just started the company,

00:06:33.116 --> 00:06:35.476
and we had this idea of a social network and things like that.

00:06:35.556 --> 00:06:36.896
I think we were like, "Okay, this is working,

00:06:37.116 --> 00:06:39.636
but I wonder if we should go back and, like,

00:06:39.936 --> 00:06:41.876
really think about the social network aspect of this."

00:06:42.156 --> 00:06:43.556
And so we tried to figure

00:06:43.616 --> 00:06:45.596
out how to get people to post on the platform

00:06:45.636 --> 00:06:48.956
and things like that until we essentially rediscovered Captions.

00:06:48.976 --> 00:06:50.596
And this was a interesting moment

00:06:50.656 --> 00:06:52.836
where we were working on another application,

00:06:52.936 --> 00:06:54.176
which was called Spam App.

00:06:54.256 --> 00:06:55.596
It was like a photo sharing app.

00:06:55.656 --> 00:06:56.676
It was actually doing really well.

00:06:56.936 --> 00:06:59.056
While we were working on that, we're looking at the bills,

00:06:59.136 --> 00:07:02.336
and there was like a good $10,000 a month going to Captions.

00:07:02.576 --> 00:07:04.376
You know, I remember talking to my co-founder and we were like,

00:07:04.476 --> 00:07:05.956
"Okay, let's just delete it

00:07:06.016 --> 00:07:09.776
because we don't wanna spend $120,000 a year on this," right?

00:07:10.076 --> 00:07:10.956
We decided to delete it.

00:07:11.196 --> 00:07:15.736
I remember going back home and on Saturday going through like,

00:07:15.936 --> 00:07:18.656
you know, the UI to be like, "Okay, I'm gonna press delete now."

00:07:18.976 --> 00:07:21.616
And while I was going to press delete, I was just thinking,

00:07:22.196 --> 00:07:24.516
"Ah, but, like, we worked so hard on this.

00:07:24.556 --> 00:07:25.776
Like, I don't wanna delete this," right?

00:07:25.816 --> 00:07:29.076
Like it's like I wanna keep it for memories at least, right?

00:07:29.556 --> 00:07:31.836
So I had an idea just in the moment,

00:07:31.996 --> 00:07:33.816
quickly designed a little paywall

00:07:33.896 --> 00:07:36.176
where we asked people to pay for the app.

00:07:36.436 --> 00:07:38.676
If nobody pays for it, then it's essentially shut down,

00:07:39.136 --> 00:07:40.996
but we can continue using it because,

00:07:41.056 --> 00:07:42.176
you know, we worked hard on it.

00:07:42.316 --> 00:07:44.636
And if someone pays for it, then it pays for itself,

00:07:45.056 --> 00:07:47.156
so we're not paying the bills basically.

00:07:47.456 --> 00:07:50.116
So I quickly made that in about two or three hours,

00:07:50.676 --> 00:07:52.156
put it on the App Store and launched it,

00:07:52.696 --> 00:07:54.056
and then completely forgot about it.

00:07:54.416 --> 00:07:56.276
So we continued working on Spam App,

00:07:56.336 --> 00:07:58.856
and it continued growing through the next,

00:07:59.056 --> 00:08:01.216
you know, four, five, six months.

00:08:01.636 --> 00:08:03.316
While we were doing that, I

00:08:03.376 --> 00:08:05.856
for some reason decided to check my account.

00:08:06.136 --> 00:08:10.056
And when I checked it, it was like at $500,000 or more and growing.

00:08:10.216 --> 00:08:13.576
And, like, the craziest part was not only did people pay for it,

00:08:13.796 --> 00:08:14.796
people retained on it,

00:08:15.376 --> 00:08:17.356
and it was growing in revenue completely

00:08:17.396 --> 00:08:19.936
on its own with like zero employees working on it.

00:08:20.156 --> 00:08:24.356
So no employees, there were like 2,000 open support tickets.

00:08:24.896 --> 00:08:27.376
Nobody was answering obviously, right?

00:08:27.676 --> 00:08:29.256
And it would continue to grow.

00:08:29.556 --> 00:08:32.416
You know, we had to have this, like, tough conversation of like,

00:08:32.836 --> 00:08:35.836
"Oh my God, like this is truly working," right?

00:08:36.316 --> 00:08:39.576
And it was tough because we already had another thing that was also

00:08:39.636 --> 00:08:42.856
working and that we were raising a new round on essentially, right?

00:08:43.116 --> 00:08:45.596
I think we discussed this for a couple of weeks,

00:08:45.856 --> 00:08:49.016
and we ended up deciding we're gonna switch to Captions

00:08:49.116 --> 00:08:51.776
and we're gonna just focus on creation as the primary.

00:08:52.376 --> 00:08:56.696
We switched over, and literally the moment we launched

00:08:56.756 --> 00:08:59.576
the first set of features on top of the existing app,

00:08:59.936 --> 00:09:03.916
what was looking like a fast growth completely condensed

00:09:04.056 --> 00:09:07.896
because the new growth was exponential, just like straight up.

00:09:08.136 --> 00:09:10.276
I mean, I think that's kind of the crazy thing that I think

00:09:10.356 --> 00:09:13.776
about at this point, is like that split second decision

00:09:14.196 --> 00:09:16.556
was the difference between two completely different paths.

00:09:16.696 --> 00:09:17.776
Here's the thing, like we don't know.

00:09:17.956 --> 00:09:19.876
The other side could have been even more successful.

00:09:19.976 --> 00:09:21.656
That's the tough part about these decisions,

00:09:21.696 --> 00:09:22.896
is like you have to choose

00:09:23.016 --> 00:09:25.036
one and you'll never know what the other could have been.

00:09:25.376 --> 00:09:26.536
I think the big decisions

00:09:26.576 --> 00:09:28.936
are actually in the small decisions in a way,

00:09:29.136 --> 00:09:30.416
and the small decisions are what

00:09:30.536 --> 00:09:32.676
actually you don't think about too much,

00:09:32.956 --> 00:09:36.616
but actually turn the tide of like what will happen in the future.

00:09:36.956 --> 00:09:38.516
I think that was the case over here too, right?

00:09:38.576 --> 00:09:41.156
Because, like, what felt like a very small decision of like other

00:09:41.256 --> 00:09:43.816
app, should we delete it or not, seemed like kind of obvious.

00:09:43.896 --> 00:09:45.036
Anybody we would've asked would've

00:09:45.076 --> 00:09:46.296
told us, "Yeah, just delete it," right?

00:09:46.456 --> 00:09:46.756
Whatever.

00:09:47.136 --> 00:09:48.656
But I think those small decisions

00:09:48.736 --> 00:09:51.936
actually are way more important than the big ones sometimes.

00:09:52.216 --> 00:09:53.496
And I think this shows quite a bit,

00:09:53.556 --> 00:09:56.256
like if you look at any competition at a global scale, right?

00:09:56.336 --> 00:09:59.256
Which building a startup is kind of a competition at a global scale.

00:09:59.556 --> 00:10:00.996
Like look at the Olympics, for example, right?

00:10:01.056 --> 00:10:03.356
Like people are like within seconds of each other, right?

00:10:03.536 --> 00:10:04.996
Sometimes even milliseconds of each other.

00:10:05.296 --> 00:10:06.936
That's not because of some major

00:10:06.976 --> 00:10:08.976
decision they made while training or something like that.

00:10:09.036 --> 00:10:10.136
Everyone's very good, right?

00:10:10.536 --> 00:10:13.196
It's probably like some minor micro decision that happened

00:10:13.236 --> 00:10:15.656
in the moment like, you know, while they're swimming

00:10:15.716 --> 00:10:18.636
and they moved one arm at this angle versus the other angle.

00:10:18.676 --> 00:10:20.776
Like that's what's deciding who wins and loses, right?

00:10:21.036 --> 00:10:23.176
I think it's like those small decisions that count the most.

00:10:29.356 --> 00:10:31.736
We did face challenges around like identifying the user

00:10:31.776 --> 00:10:33.576
and making sure we're building for the right user.

00:10:33.816 --> 00:10:35.416
Because when we launched the app,

00:10:35.476 --> 00:10:37.796
like it was immediately sort of successful, right?

00:10:37.856 --> 00:10:39.536
And it immediately blew up.

00:10:39.916 --> 00:10:42.556
It made things easier and harder in some ways.

00:10:42.676 --> 00:10:45.296
I think one of the things that can happen when too many people

00:10:45.376 --> 00:10:49.916
join sort of your app or product very quickly is you kind of lose,

00:10:50.296 --> 00:10:52.136
lose sight of who the customer is because

00:10:52.156 --> 00:10:53.796
there's a lot of people who are just checking it out.

00:10:53.916 --> 00:10:56.856
There's a lot of people who don't actually care about the use case,

00:10:56.916 --> 00:10:58.316
but they wanna just see what's going on.

00:10:58.536 --> 00:11:00.536
Between all that noise, it's difficult to figure out like

00:11:00.596 --> 00:11:03.736
which one of these people are the actual user, who's gonna retain,

00:11:03.776 --> 00:11:06.156
and for whom is this actually solving a real problem?

00:11:06.456 --> 00:11:08.116
That's really important because at the end of the day,

00:11:08.696 --> 00:11:10.296
without focusing on the right customer,

00:11:10.356 --> 00:11:12.036
you'll end up building the wrong product.

00:11:12.296 --> 00:11:13.656
Captions was a paid app from the beginning,

00:11:13.816 --> 00:11:15.956
and I think elements there that was interesting

00:11:15.996 --> 00:11:19.276
is like being paid actually helped filter our user base

00:11:19.336 --> 00:11:22.196
a little bit into only those people who were willing to pay.

00:11:22.456 --> 00:11:23.616
Those people that were willing to pay

00:11:23.676 --> 00:11:25.756
were people who had real pain points.

00:11:25.816 --> 00:11:28.896
And so with that, we got really good feedback from users, right?

00:11:29.016 --> 00:11:31.296
Around like what we should build and what we should prioritize

00:11:31.316 --> 00:11:32.676
and what they want from us in the future.

00:11:33.156 --> 00:11:36.016
We decided to keep the app paid right from the beginning

00:11:36.096 --> 00:11:37.976
all the way till today because of that reason.

00:11:38.596 --> 00:11:41.656
I think on the technical side we try to avoid as much as possible

00:11:41.876 --> 00:11:44.756
technical challenges that are sort of unproven essentially, right?

00:11:44.796 --> 00:11:46.976
Like I think one of the mistakes that can be made is like spending

00:11:47.016 --> 00:11:49.976
a lot of time building something very technically complex without

00:11:50.036 --> 00:11:53.036
fully recognizing whether there's product market fit there or not.

00:11:53.076 --> 00:11:55.376
We spend a year building something and nobody wants it, right?

00:11:55.536 --> 00:11:57.376
That happens very often, and it's happened to us too.

00:11:57.616 --> 00:11:59.096
The key factor there is like

00:11:59.156 --> 00:12:01.776
how do you reduce the technical risk to like an MVP?

00:12:01.956 --> 00:12:03.836
How do you test with like just the bare minimum,

00:12:04.016 --> 00:12:05.236
get something out in front of people,

00:12:05.336 --> 00:12:06.636
have them see it, have them try it?

00:12:06.836 --> 00:12:09.076
If they don't retain, then don't invest more in it, right?

00:12:09.296 --> 00:12:09.956
Try something else.

00:12:10.296 --> 00:12:12.836
And I think that's the other thing, is like not going deeper

00:12:12.896 --> 00:12:15.036
and deeper into the rabbit hole almost in a way.

00:12:15.476 --> 00:12:18.396
I think there's also tendencies of like building the first version,

00:12:18.876 --> 00:12:20.376
seeing that it doesn't completely work,

00:12:20.436 --> 00:12:22.616
and then justifying it by saying, "Well,

00:12:22.696 --> 00:12:25.476
if it only had this feature and that feature and that third feature,

00:12:25.896 --> 00:12:27.276
then maybe it would've worked," right?

00:12:27.396 --> 00:12:29.816
But I think the reality is like it would not, probably

00:12:29.836 --> 00:12:32.096
not have worked with as many features as you want.

00:12:32.476 --> 00:12:33.716
It would only have worked with a single

00:12:33.776 --> 00:12:35.176
feature, only one of those things, right?

00:12:35.296 --> 00:12:37.576
Whatever actually people want at the end of the day.

00:12:37.936 --> 00:12:39.636
So I think the better skill is to just learn

00:12:39.656 --> 00:12:42.656
how to quickly build stuff, try as many things as possible.

00:12:42.716 --> 00:12:44.536
The most important thing is getting

00:12:44.576 --> 00:12:46.416
stuff out in front of people, testing it.

00:12:46.496 --> 00:12:48.536
If it doesn't work, kill it and start again,

00:12:48.596 --> 00:12:50.536
and keep doing that as quickly as possible.

